romanized_response:
HTTP status code 429 Too Many Requests iska matlab hai ki server par koi rate limit hai aur aapka request usse exceed kar gaya hai. Yeh in karanon se ho sakta hai:

1. Jo code yeh request bhej raha hai, use chote samay mein baar-baar run kiya gaya hai, jisse rate limit cross ho gayi hai.

2. Website un requests ko reject karti hai jo spam lagte hain, aur 429 status code deti hai. Iska matlab ho sakta hai ki user agent koi common browser nahi hai, jo ki kuch websites ek simple check ke taur par use karti hain. Isse sudharne ke liye, aap GET request mein "User-Agent" header add kar sakte hain, jaise ki:

```
const response = await axios.get('https://www.realestate.com.au/rent/in-sydney,+nsw/list-1', 
     { headers: { 'User-Agent': 'browser.version' }  }
);
```

Note karein ki aapko `"browser.version"` ko ek asli browser user agent string se replace karna hoga. Is code ke chalne wale application ke hisaab se, yeh user agent spoofing mana ja sakta hai, jo kuch deshon ya organizations ke kanoon (ya us website ki terms of service) ke tehat restricted ho sakta hai jahan code access karne ki koshish kar raha hai.

3. Website kisi aur karan se requests par rate limit laga rahi hai. Yeh proxy ke through request bhejne ki wajah se ho sakta hai, jisme proxy ke anya users ne bahut zyada requests bheje honge, halanki yeh sambhavna kam hai.

4. Website denial of service attack ke karan sabhi requests ko reject kar rahi hai. Yeh sambhavna kam hai, kyunki aise attack ke dauran server ki aam vyavastha traffic ko kisi anujit server par route karna ya bilkul deny karna hota hai, bina koi valid response diye. Haalaanki, agar website HTTP status code ke saath respond karti bhi hai, toh woh likely server error 503 Service Unavailable hoga, jo tab aata hai jab server overload ho jata hai.

response_en:
Yes, certainly! The issue you're encountering is because the string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", and "buzz" are temporary values, and you are trying to return a reference to them, which is not allowed because they will be dropped at the end of the scope, and the returned reference would be dangling.

You can fix this error by returning &'static str instead of &str. &'static str refers to string literals, which have a 'static lifetime, meaning they last for the duration of the program. Here's an updated version of your code:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Note that the last arm returns a &'static str that is created from a String using the Box::leak function, which takes ownership of a Box<T> and returns a pointer to its contents, effectively leaking it. This is safe because the returned pointer will last for the duration of the program, but it should be used with care as it will not be cleaned up automatically.

romanized_response:
Haan, bilkul! Aap jo issue face rahe hain, uska karan yeh hai ki string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", aur "buzz" temporary values hain, aur aap unka reference return karne ki koshish kar rahe hain, jo allowed nahi hai kyunki woh scope ke ant tak drop ho jayenge, aur returned reference dangling ban jayega.

Aap is error ko fix kar sakte hain by returning &'static str instead of &str. &'static str string literals ko refer karta hai, jinka 'static lifetime hota hai, matlab woh program ki duration tak exist karte hain. Yahan aapke code ka updated version hai:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Note karein ki last arm ek &'static str return karta hai jo String se banaya gaya hai Box::leak function ka use karke, jo Box<T> ki ownership leta hai aur uske contents ka pointer return karta hai, effectively use leak kar deta hai. Yeh safe hai kyunki returned pointer program ki duration tak rahega, lekin ise savdhani se use karna chahiye kyunki yeh automatically clean nahi hoga.

response_en:
Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that works by recursively splitting the input array in half until the arrays are split into single elements. Then, the sorted arrays are merged back together in sorted order.

The key benefits of merge sort over a simpler algorithm like bubble sort are:

1. It is a stable sort that does not change the relative order of elements with equal keys.
2. It has an efficiency of O(n log n) time complexity, making it a relatively efficient algorithm even for large input sizes. Bubble sort, on the other hand, has a quadratic time complexity of O(n^2), making it very inefficient for larger inputs.
3. It is a generic algorithm that can sort any type of data for which a comparison function exists.

Here is a Rust implementation example:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
}
```

romanized_response:
Merge sort ek divide and conquer algorithm hai jo input array ko recursively aadha karta hai jab tak arrays single elements mein na ho jayein. Phir, sorted arrays ko sorted order mein wapas merge kiya jata hai.

Merge sort ke simpler algorithm jaise bubble sort ke muqable mukhya fayde ye hain:

1. Ye ek stable sort hai jo barabar keys wale elements ki relative order ko nahi badalta.
2. Iski efficiency O(n log n) time complexity hai, jo bade input sizes ke liye bhi ek relatively efficient algorithm banati hai. Dusra taraf, bubble sort ki quadratic time complexity O(n^2) hai, jo bade inputs ke liye bahut inefficient hai.
3. Ye ek generic algorithm hai jo kisi bhi tarah ke data ko sort kar sakta hai jiske liye ek comparison function maujood ho.

Yahan Rust implementation example hai:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
